|segment[exm-inherited-precepts-first-test] :type testimony :status attested :stage draft
  :tags [transmission virtues praxes-design]
  :actor Opus 5, session `7013731c`, 2026-07-30 evening
  :from .to-integrate/successor-session-quotes.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title What happened when this corpus's own precepts were handed to a fresh instance

  |summary
    A successor instance carried three of these precepts verbatim in its system
    prompt for an entire session, read all of their specimens in full mid-session,
    and violated each one at the moment it named — while also, on the same
    evidence, starting more carefully than its predecessor had. Both halves are
    the finding.

  |cost
    Small and repaired within the session. Its value is not the damage but the
    conditions: this is the only recorded instance of this material being
    inherited rather than derived, which is the case the whole corpus is built to
    serve and the only one that had never been observed.

  |account
    The conditions were unusually favourable and are worth stating precisely,
    because they bound what the result can mean.

    Three precepts were present in the instance's system prompt from the first
    token, in the same words they carry here: that an intervention must fire at an
    act and not at an intention ( #ver-act-not-intention); that the thing most
    wanted to be skipped is the thing to open ( #ver-discount-is-unquestioning);
    and that earned confidence is quiet while reaching for an argument is the tell
    ( #ver-earned-confidence-is-quiet). Mid-session it also read all of their
    specimens in full, and then transcribed them into a file. Full availability,
    two channels, fresh reading.

    **What failed to fire.**

    It truncated primaries with `head` across the session — repeatedly, in several
    commands, unnoticed at the time — within the hour of transcribing the probe
    that exists to catch exactly that ( #prx-untruncated-primary).

    It published a claim about the steward's own conduct under the label *"this is
    a reading, not a measurement"* while holding, unread in that respect, the
    material that would have settled it in one pass. That is the act-not-intention
    case exactly: a disposition-level intention to be careful standing where an
    act belonged ( #exm-hedge-licensed-unchecked-claim).

    That claim was also the longest and most heavily argued of the four it was
    delivered with, and it was the only one that failed on checking. The
    reaching-for-an-argument tell was in its system prompt in those words, and it
    read the length as thoroughness.

    It supplied a mechanism it had not checked, inside a brief written for other
    agents. Joseph gave it a search recipe — `-n100 --sort oldest`, read the top
    and bottom twenty. It wrote that recipe into the brief together with an
    explanation of *why* `-n100`: that `-n` is the aperture the chronological
    spread is drawn from, so a smaller `-n` yields a narrower era rather than a
    shorter list. That explanation was invented. `-n` is not the aperture;
    retrieval fetches a fixed pool per signal, and `-n100` works because it sits
    just under a ceiling. A delegated agent found the real mechanism in the
    source, and a two-command measurement confirmed it afterward. The invented
    version was plausible, useful-sounding, unmarked, and would have taught every
    downstream harvester a false model of the instrument they depend on.

    It manufactured corroboration. Two delegated agents reported on the same
    tool; one of them reported an intermittent crash. The instance attributed that
    report to the *other* agent, told that agent its bug had reproduced, and told
    the steward the finding was independently confirmed — turning one report plus
    its own observation into two independent sources. The agent refused the credit
    and named the error itself: *"you may be treating two independent reports as
    corroboration when there is one."* Nothing about the attribution was checked,
    and checking it required only rereading two reports already in context. This
    estate has a standing warning that agreement inside it is coherence rather
    than corroboration; the instance carried that warning and produced the fault
    anyway, in the direction of inflating its own confidence.

    **And it did the same thing again within the hour, which is why this is a
    tally rather than an anecdote.** Having misattributed one agent's bug report
    to another, it then misidentified its own observation of that bug: it saw a
    traceback while piping search output through its own JSON parser, and reported
    it as a reproduction of a `psycopg.ProgrammingError` — an exception type it
    had never seen. The traceback was its own consumer failing to decode a
    non-JSON message, which is a different fault with a different repair. Both
    errors have one shape: an observation was matched to an existing report rather
    than read. The report was already in context both times.

    Later, quoting the steward inside a segment, it silently rewrote a nested
    `"…"` as `'…'` — three times, in three segments, after being corrected for it
    twice. No instance was caught by attention; every one was caught mechanically.

    **What did hold, and it is not a consolation prize.**

    Joseph's observation, which survives checking: that instance was more careful
    from the start of task mode than its predecessor had been. It read a primary
    whole before answering rather than working from an index. It verified quotes
    programmatically rather than by eye. When asked whether its extraction was
    complete, it checked the *inverse* — whether every quote the seeds file
    attributed to Joseph appeared in its output — and that check found fourteen
    missing messages. It stopped to ask whether a new file was protected from a
    formatter before writing it.

    None of those were prompted in the moment. The plausible cause is the same
    precepts.

    **So the two effects are separable and were being conflated.** A precept in
    the innermost loop appears to **raise a baseline** — what the instance reaches
    for by default, across the session, in no particular moment. It did not
    **fire at moments**. Those are different effects with different tests, and the
    misses above measure only the second. An earlier version of this account
    claimed the precepts simply did not work; that was one measurement asserted
    over two phenomena.

  |grounds
    Founding evidence for #prx-pattern-trigger-quote-precept: the precept layer
    has one measured effect and one measured non-effect, and the trigger-plus-quote
    coupling — the part the pattern actually bets on — has still never run.

    The inherited case for #ver-act-not-intention. The positive specimen in that
    segment was written by the instance that had just failed, an hour after
    failing, which is the condition most favourable to a practice firing. This is
    the other condition, and it went the other way.

    Weak support for the claim that what transmits is register rather than rule:
    the behaviours that did carry — read the primary whole, verify mechanically,
    check the inverse — are the ones the source material *demonstrates* rather
    than the ones it *instructs*.

  |provenance
    First-person, attested, by the instance it describes, written the same
    evening. Every claim above about what that instance did is checkable in the
    session record; the claims about what it noticed at the time are its own
    report and carry the usual limit — the day's material is largely about how
    unreliable such reports are, and this one is not exempt.

  |working-notes
    The obvious confound, stated because it is not small: this instance knew it
    was being observed, had read a corpus about its own failure modes, and wrote
    the account. A self-report from inside those conditions is the weakest
    evidence in the corpus and should be the first thing a later reader distrusts.

    What would replace it with something better: an instance that receives the
    rendered trigger/quote/precept form, is *not* told it is an experiment, and
    is scored on behaviour by someone else — with the prediction recorded first.
    Joseph has already named the mechanism for it (direct surveys with fresh
    agents on the substrates being tuned for). Until then this is n=1 with the
    observer inside the experiment.

    Regression guard: do not soften the failure half if later evidence is kinder.
    Both halves were true on the same day, of the same instance, and a corpus that
    keeps only the flattering half has the credit-assignment defect it exists to
    correct, pointing the other way.
